From: churyakovis <355423536+churyakovis@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: sched: act_mirred: do not push a mac header on L3 ingress

tcf_mirred_to_dev() pushes skb->mac_len bytes when the target expects the
data at the MAC header. On ingress from an L3 device (raw-IP wwan, tunnels,
PPP) there is no L2 header in front of the network header:
__netif_receive_skb_core() runs skb_reset_mac_len(), which computes mac_len
as network_header - mac_header, i.e. the offset of the data from the head of
the buffer rather than a header length. Pushing that many bytes walks the
data pointer back to (or past) the head, and the next skb_push() in the
transmit path panics with skb_under_panic.

Seen on a UZ801 v3 (6.12.112) with

  tc qdisc add dev wwan0 clsact
  tc filter add dev wwan0 ingress matchall \
     action mirred egress redirect dev ifb0

and inbound LTE traffic: skb_under_panic and a reboot, with or without
CAKE; egress-only CAKE was fine.

Ask the device the action is coming from, not the one it is going to:
dev_is_mac_header_xmit() is the predicate already used for the target, and
it is false for ARPHRD_NONE/RAWIP/tunnels/PPP. An L3 source therefore
contributes no header. Also refuse a push that would not fit in the
headroom - dropping one packet is strictly better than panicking.

Reported by churyakovis in #84, who also wrote and tested the first version
of this fix on a UZ801 v3 (ingress-only IFB, then CAKE in both directions).
The source-device predicate and the headroom guard here are a re-key of it.
---
diff --git a/net/sched/act_mirred.c b/net/sched/act_mirred.c
--- a/net/sched/act_mirred.c
+++ b/net/sched/act_mirred.c
@@ -296,13 +296,34 @@
 	expects_nh = want_ingress || !m_mac_header_xmit;
 	at_nh = skb->data == skb_network_header(skb);
 	if (at_nh != expects_nh) {
-		mac_len = at_ingress ? skb->mac_len :
-			  skb_network_offset(skb);
+		if (at_ingress && !dev_is_mac_header_xmit(skb->dev)) {
+			/*
+			 * L3 ingress (raw-IP wwan, tunnels, PPP): there is no L2
+			 * header in front of the network header, so skb->mac_len
+			 * holds the offset of the data from the head of the
+			 * buffer, not a header length. Pushing it moves the data
+			 * pointer back to (or past) the head and the xmit path
+			 * then panics in skb_push().
+			 */
+			mac_len = 0;
+		} else {
+			mac_len = at_ingress ? skb->mac_len :
+				  skb_network_offset(skb);
+		}
 		if (expects_nh) {
 			/* target device/action expect data at nh */
 			skb_pull_rcsum(skb_to_send, mac_len);
 		} else {
 			/* target device/action expect data at mac */
+			if (unlikely((unsigned int)mac_len >
+				     skb_headroom(skb_to_send))) {
+				net_warn_ratelimited("%s: mac_len %d exceeds headroom %u, dropping\n",
+						     skb->dev->name, mac_len,
+						     skb_headroom(skb_to_send));
+				if (skb_to_send != skb)
+					kfree_skb(skb_to_send);
+				goto err_cant_do;
+			}
 			skb_push_rcsum(skb_to_send, mac_len);
 		}
 	}
